@page "/privacy"

@* What the app stores and why, as the code does it. Keep this page in step
   when a feature starts storing something new or sending data somewhere new. *@

<PageTitle>Privacy · @Brand.AppName</PageTitle>

<article class="legal">
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="muted">Last updated @LastUpdated</p>

    <p>
        This policy explains what @Brand.AppName keeps about you, why, who else sees any of it, and how you can get a copy
        or have it deleted.
        @if (Brand.Operator != Brand.AppName)
        {
            <span> @Brand.AppName is run by @Brand.Operator ("we").</span>
        }
        @if (Brand.ContactEmail is { } email)
        {
            <span> Questions go to <a href="mailto:@email">@email</a>.</span>
        }
    </p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>We keep what you tell the app (your shows, the episodes you've watched, your ratings and posts) so it can work
            for you. We don't sell it, and there are no advertising trackers.</li>
        <li>Figures about our users that others can see, such as how many people finished a season, need at least five
            people behind them, so they can't point to you.</li>
        <li>You can download everything we hold about you, and delete your account, in Settings at any time.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we keep</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Your account:</strong> your username, email address and password (held by our sign-in service, with the
            password stored only as a hash), and your profile: display name, picture address, time zone, region and
            language.</li>
        <li><strong>Your viewing:</strong> the shows you follow and their status, the episodes you mark as watched and
            when, your lists, your ratings of shows and episodes, your episode duels and your predictions.</li>
        <li><strong>What you write for others:</strong> comments, tips and community insights, suggested corrections,
            flags on other people's posts, and the helpful votes you give.</li>
        <li><strong>People:</strong> who you follow, who follows you, follow requests, the people you've blocked, and your
            privacy settings.</li>
        <li><strong>Notifications:</strong> your notification preferences, your inbox, and the devices you've allowed to
            receive push notifications.</li>
        <li><strong>Plans and payments:</strong> your plan, your family plan's members, your subscription (plan, price,
            period, whether it renews) and a receipt for each payment. Your card details go to the payment provider and
            never reach us.</li>
        <li><strong>History you import:</strong> the file is read on your device; only the shows, episodes and dates it
            contains are sent to us, and account files in an export archive are never opened.</li>
        <li><strong>Streaming services</strong> you tell us you pay for, to show what they cost you per hour watched.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
    <p>
        Some settings never leave the device you set them on: the theme, the spoiler shield, your birth year (for Your TV
        life), your birthday (for Birthdays) and your Guess the show record. The web app keeps them in your browser's
        storage, the phone app in its own preferences.
    </p>

    <h2>Why we use it</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>To run the service you signed up for: tracking, calendars, recommendations, insights and the social
            features. This is the basis for nearly everything we keep.</li>
        <li>To take payments and keep the records the law requires.</li>
        <li>To keep the service safe: rate limits, moderation of posts, and logs we use to find faults.</li>
        <li>To send the notifications and the weekly digest you've turned on. You can turn each one off in Settings.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Our insights are computed from the episodes and ratings of shows, and from your own viewing for the ones about
        you. Where an insight is about other users, we show it only with five or more people or ratings behind it.
    </p>

    <h2>What others can see</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>People who follow you see your activity (episodes watched, shows followed, finished and rated) unless you
            turn off "share my activity". A private profile shows only your name to anyone you haven't approved.</li>
        <li>Your comments and other posts are public to signed-in users, under your display name. Episode titles are never
            shown in the activity feed, only episode numbers.</li>
        <li>A list you share is visible on your profile; a list link can be opened by anyone who has it, until you turn
            the link off.</li>
        <li>Family plan members see the owner's name and each other's names, nothing more.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who else receives data</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Your payment provider</strong> (Stripe) handles checkout and billing, including your card and billing
            details, under its own privacy policy. We tell it which plan you're buying; it tells us when a payment
            succeeds.</li>
        <li><strong>Streaming services</strong> you open from a show page: the link goes through us so we can count clicks
            per service, without recording who clicked. Some links carry an affiliate code, so the service may pay us a
            commission on a sign-up.</li>
        <li><strong>Show pictures</strong> are loaded by your browser or phone from @Brand.CatalogImagesFrom, which therefore sees your
            device's network address, as any website you visit does.</li>
        <li>Our show data comes from @(Brand.CatalogSource == "wikidata" ? "Wikidata and Wikiquote" : $"{Brand.CatalogSourceName}, Wikidata and Wikiquote"). We fetch it ourselves, and send them nothing about
            you; when you import a history, the titles of shows we don't yet have are looked up there, without your
            name.</li>
        <li>We use hosting and infrastructure providers to run the service, who process data only on our behalf.</li>
        <li>We may disclose data where the law requires it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Cookies</h2>
    <p>
        The web app uses one cookie to keep you signed in, and the sign-in service sets its own while you sign in. There
        are no advertising or analytics cookies.
    </p>

    <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Your account and what you've added stay until you delete them or your account.</li>
        <li>Notifications are removed 90 days after you've read them, and after a year at the latest.</li>
        <li>Payment receipts are kept while your account exists; the payment provider keeps its own records for as long
            as the law requires.</li>
        <li>Counts of clicks on streaming links carry no information about who clicked.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>A copy of your data:</strong> Settings → Export my data gives you everything every part of the app
            holds about you, as a file.</li>
        <li><strong>Deleting your account:</strong> Settings → Delete account removes your account and everything you've
            added, from every part of the app, and stops any subscription at once. Posts you wrote go with it.</li>
        <li><strong>Correcting it:</strong> you can change your profile, ratings, lists and posts at any time.</li>
        <li>Depending on where you live (for example under the GDPR or POPIA), you may also have the right to object to or
            restrict how we use your data, and to complain to your data protection authority.
            @if (Brand.ContactEmail is { } address)
            {
                <span> Write to <a href="mailto:@address">@address</a> for anything Settings doesn't cover.</span>
            }
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
        @Brand.AppName isn't meant for children under 13, or under the age at which you can agree to this yourself where
        you live (16 in parts of Europe). We don't knowingly keep their data, and delete it when we learn of it.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
        When we change this policy we'll update the date above, and tell you in the app before a change that affects
        what we do with data you've already given us.
    </p>

    <p class="muted"><a href="terms">Terms of use</a></p>
</article>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// When the policy last changed; update it with any change to the text.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string LastUpdated = "9 November 2026";
}
